1
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:04,270
Hello, English learners.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,880
Welcome back to another great podcast here at EnglishPod.

3
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:08,270
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:09,090
And I'm Erica.

5
00:00:09,590 --> 00:00:11,770
And today we're going to be bringing you bad news.

6
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:13,130
That's right.

7
00:00:13,510 --> 00:00:17,350
Actually, what we're really going to be bringing you is language to talk about bad news.

8
00:00:17,930 --> 00:00:18,290
Exactly.

9
00:00:18,630 --> 00:00:25,440
So maybe you have to give bad news to your boss, to your parents, I don't know, somebody, and we're going to be teaching you how to do this in English.

10
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:31,140
Well, obviously, it's always difficult to tell somebody bad news, but in your second language, it's even harder.

11
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:35,200
So we're going to try and help you today with some great phrases and vocabulary items.

12
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,100
We're not going to preview anything today.

13
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:45,200
Let's go directly to our dialogue where we will be in a company and one of the employees is going to be giving bad news to his boss.

14
00:00:45,580 --> 00:00:46,440
So let's listen.

15
00:00:54,250 --> 00:01:00,050
Now that we've been over the gory details of our disastrous first quarter, Ed, give us some good news.

16
00:01:00,590 --> 00:01:02,310
How are things looking for us in terms of sales this month?

17
00:01:03,310 --> 00:01:06,870
Well, would you like the bad news first or the really bad news?

18
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:08,560
What?

19
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:10,230
Ed, don't tell me you only have bad news.

20
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:16,150
Well, sir, our sales have dropped, no plunged, 50% in the past month alone.

21
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:20,010
We are currently overstocked and overstaffed and our profits are falling fast.

22
00:01:20,730 --> 00:01:24,750
The market is in recession and we have no way of moving our inventory or getting rid of our staff.

23
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:30,980
If we consider redundancies, it costs us a fortune because of the new regulations governing compensation packages.

24
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:32,220
It's a real mess.

25
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:33,760
You're crying out loud.

26
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:34,520
How fast are we losing money?

27
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:37,540
How can I put this?

28
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:42,860
Let's just say that at this pace, we will be filing for Chapter 11 in less than three months.

29
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:44,920
What?

30
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:45,860
Jeez, how could this have happened?

31
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:47,940
So what's the bad news?

32
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:49,780
Oh, that's the really bad news.

33
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:54,080
Our supplier suffered QC problems and, well, half of our production is faulty.

34
00:01:54,840 --> 00:02:00,220
We're going to have to recall all items sold in the last quarter and the worst part, we're going to have to shoulder this cost.

35
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:01,500
Are you joking?

36
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,440
Get the supplier on the line now.

37
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:05,960
They have to assume the cost of this mess.

38
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:07,260
We tried that, sir.

39
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:10,940
The factory has gone under and the owner apparently has fled the country.

40
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:11,720
Oh, we're doomed.

41
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:13,820
There's some really good news, though.

42
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:15,440
Really?

43
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:16,440
What?

44
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:17,440
I got offered a new job.

45
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:25,040
All right, it sounds like a pretty tough time for this company, doesn't it?

46
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:25,910
But good news for the employee.

47
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:27,030
He won't be jobless.

48
00:02:27,490 --> 00:02:29,010
Well, I guess he knows when to get out.

49
00:02:29,370 --> 00:02:33,150
But why don't we take a look at some of the language now in Language Takeaway.

50
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:42,760
So in Language Takeaway today, we have five vocabulary items and, well, let's start with the first one.

51
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:50,180
All right, so the boss, he's talking about the gory details of our disastrous first quarter.

52
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:52,620
So let's look at the phrase gory details.

53
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:54,780
All right, so gory details.

54
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:55,680
Gory details.

55
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:58,080
This sounds kind of strange, gory details.

56
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,220
It seems very scary.

57
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:03,560
Well, the word gory comes from gore, like blood and guts, right?

58
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:03,960
Exactly.

59
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:06,440
That's why it sounds a little bit scary to me.

60
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:15,480
So gory details mean all the little details of a negative or shocking or bad situation.

61
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:20,510
Okay, so the gory details of the meeting, so all the nasty negative stuff.

62
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:25,330
Or the gory detail, or you could say the newspaper reported all the gory details of the murder.

63
00:03:25,710 --> 00:03:27,290
Okay, very good.

64
00:03:27,390 --> 00:03:28,370
So gory details.

65
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:34,790
So the employee was talking about how bad the company was doing and he mentioned this word redundancies.

66
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:36,680
Redundancies.

67
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:38,720
They are considering redundancies.

68
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:43,800
Okay, so redundancies, again, it's based on the word to be redundant.

69
00:03:43,970 --> 00:03:45,750
All right, and that means?

70
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:47,090
To not be needed anymore.

71
00:03:47,450 --> 00:03:50,590
Okay, so redundancies are basically firing people.

72
00:03:50,850 --> 00:03:53,670
It's well, laying people off, people that you don't need anymore.

73
00:03:53,890 --> 00:03:55,250
They are redundancies.

74
00:03:55,810 --> 00:04:03,250
All right, so this is actually a British term, redundancies, to become redundant, because in American English, you would just say layoffs.

75
00:04:03,890 --> 00:04:04,010
Yep.

76
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:05,640
Okay, very good.

77
00:04:05,960 --> 00:04:06,780
All right, so moving on.

78
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:16,820
They were talking about redundancies and maybe they should or shouldn't lay some people off, but one of the problems were the regulations governing compensation packages.

79
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:21,620
Okay, so regulations governing compensation packages.

80
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:23,500
Regulations governing something.

81
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:25,820
So we have a noun governing noun.

82
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:27,200
What does that mean?

83
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:36,240
All right, so I think it would help us to understand this a little better if we listen to some examples of this pattern, noun governing noun.

84
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:37,240
Example one.

85
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:41,400
I don't agree with the regulations governing income taxes.

86
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:43,500
People shouldn't have to give away their hard-earned money.

87
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:48,080
Example two.

88
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:50,080
We need to protect the environment.

89
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:55,710
Therefore, there will be new policies governing corporate levels of pollution starting next month.

90
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:00,960
Example three.

91
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:06,680
The lack of regulations governing city transportation has led to many traffic accidents.

92
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:08,560
Okay, great examples.

93
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:16,760
And well, before we move on, this compensation packages, I guess it's all the things that the company gives you when they lay you off, right?

94
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:21,600
Right, maybe a certain portion of your retirement benefits or...

95
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:23,760
Like a month's pay, etc.

96
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:26,000
Okay, compensation packages.

97
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,480
All right, so moving on to our next item on Language Takeaway, we have to shoulder the cost.

98
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:33,870
To shoulder the cost.

99
00:05:34,530 --> 00:05:37,110
All right, so if I shoulder something...

100
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:38,330
You have to carry it.

101
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:39,250
I carry it.

102
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:40,640
Yeah, that's a good one.

103
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:43,190
So basically, it's the same as the shoulder, your body.

104
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:45,910
So you carry this cost or you carry this responsibility.

105
00:05:47,210 --> 00:05:49,650
So you might also hear shoulder the burden.

106
00:05:50,390 --> 00:05:51,610
To shoulder the burden.

107
00:05:52,510 --> 00:05:54,270
So it just means to carry the responsibility.

108
00:05:54,890 --> 00:05:55,970
Okay, to share it.

109
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:57,400
Yeah.

110
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:58,480
All right, interesting.

111
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:00,310
And we have one more word on Language Takeaway.

112
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:04,160
And the factory has gone under.

113
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:04,930
To go under.

114
00:06:05,410 --> 00:06:06,810
All right, to go under.

115
00:06:07,190 --> 00:06:11,570
When something goes under, a company goes under, it goes bankrupt.

116
00:06:12,110 --> 00:06:12,850
It goes bankrupt.

117
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:15,560
So it's not working anymore.

118
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:16,560
That's right.

119
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:17,670
All right, so to go under.

120
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:20,700
Great vocabulary items here on Language Takeaway today.

121
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:25,020
It's time for us to move on to the next segment of our show where we're going to look at some phrases.

122
00:06:25,640 --> 00:06:30,040
So let's start now with Fluency Builder.

123
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:30,340
Fluency Builder.

124
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:39,840
All right, so we've got some phrases that you can use when somebody is going to tell you some bad news, but also some phrases you can use to deliver bad news.

125
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:43,520
All right, so let's start with our first phrase.

126
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:49,000
So the boss is getting ready to prepare for this bad news and he says, lay it on me.

127
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:51,380
He's like, okay, lay it on me.

128
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:52,260
Lay it on me.

129
00:06:52,760 --> 00:06:55,840
So if I say this, lay it on me, I'm just saying, tell me.

130
00:06:56,040 --> 00:06:56,280
Exactly.

131
00:06:56,860 --> 00:06:57,920
So give me the bad news.

132
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:01,040
All right, so lay it on me.

133
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:02,040
Very informal though.

134
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:02,400
Yeah, you're right.

135
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:04,040
It is kind of informal.

136
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:10,500
But our next phrase, the boss is hearing about all this bad news and he says, for crying out loud.

137
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:13,040
For crying out loud.

138
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:14,520
For crying out loud.

139
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:19,840
All right, so this is a very common phrase of frustration.

140
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:24,320
So why don't we listen to some examples to kind of understand how we use this phrase.

141
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:26,290
Example one.

142
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:35,080
For crying out loud, when I tell you to have the report done by Wednesday, that means I expect it to be on my desk by the end of the day.

143
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:39,720
Example two.

144
00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:42,770
We've got to figure out a new marketing strategy for crying out loud.

145
00:07:45,080 --> 00:07:47,680
Example three.

146
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:53,760
For crying out loud, you just got your driving license and you already got in an accident.

147
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:55,560
So it kind of reminds me of, oh my God.

148
00:07:56,260 --> 00:07:59,980
So you're frustrated, you're just saying this phrase as, oh my God, or something like this.

149
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:02,480
Yeah, it's like, oh, this is so annoying.

150
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:03,200
Exactly.

151
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:05,040
For crying out loud.

152
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:06,040
Yep.

153
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:08,680
All right, moving on to our third phrase.

154
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:09,610
How can I put this?

155
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:11,050
How can I put this?

156
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:15,790
So the boss asked him a direct question and he said, well, how can I put this?

157
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:20,730
So we use this phrase, we want to say something maybe a little bit more indirectly.

158
00:08:21,470 --> 00:08:25,450
Right, so you're looking for words of how you can make it softer or more polite.

159
00:08:25,830 --> 00:08:31,170
Maybe the truth or what you're about to say is a little bit strong, so you want to soften it up a bit.

160
00:08:31,430 --> 00:08:31,690
Yeah.

161
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:34,920
Okay, so you say, how can I put this?

162
00:08:35,380 --> 00:08:40,300
But then you have to complement it with, let's just say that.

163
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:42,540
Let's just say that.

164
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:45,000
Let's just say that.

165
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:45,960
So how do we use this phrase?

166
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:49,520
So for example, somebody asks me, well, what do you mean?

167
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:51,420
Why can't you come on vacation with us?

168
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:54,240
And I would say, how can I put this?

169
00:08:54,900 --> 00:09:00,110
Let's just say that in nine months you will hear the pitter patter of little feet.

170
00:09:00,390 --> 00:09:01,850
Okay, so your girlfriend's pregnant.

171
00:09:02,150 --> 00:09:02,350
Exactly.

172
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:05,720
So you're not saying, my girlfriend is pregnant.

173
00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:06,470
You're giving like a metaphor.

174
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:09,490
A metaphor of what's going on, but you don't want to say it directly.

175
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:11,040
Yeah.

176
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:17,760
So for example, I might say, let's just say that Michael isn't doing a great job.

177
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:18,760
All right.

178
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:22,390
So maybe there are very strong reasons or he's doing very, very poorly.

179
00:09:22,870 --> 00:09:23,010
Right.

180
00:09:23,050 --> 00:09:27,740
But I'm just sort of not saying it exactly directly that he's doing bad.

181
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,080
Okay, very good.

182
00:09:29,340 --> 00:09:37,020
So it's a phrase that you can actually use a lot and to be a little bit more polite or a little bit more indirect when you're giving somebody some bad news.

183
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:37,880
Exactly.

184
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:52,400
So we have two nice sort of complimenting phrases for delivering bad news and why don't we hear them one more time in the dialogue.

185
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:57,770
Now that we've been over the gory details of our disastrous first quarter, Ed, give us some good news.

186
00:09:58,330 --> 00:10:00,050
How are things looking for us in terms of sales this month?

187
00:10:01,070 --> 00:10:04,610
Well, would you like the bad news first or the really bad news?

188
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:06,280
What?

189
00:10:06,450 --> 00:10:07,970
Don't tell me you only have bad news.

190
00:10:08,650 --> 00:10:10,390
Well, sir, our sales have dropped.

191
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:14,160
No plunged 50% in the past month alone.

192
00:10:14,550 --> 00:10:17,770
We are currently overstocked and overstaffed and our profits are falling fast.

193
00:10:18,470 --> 00:10:22,510
The market is in recession and we have no way of moving our inventory or getting rid of our staff.

194
00:10:22,990 --> 00:10:28,710
If we consider redundancies, it costs us a fortune because of the new regulations governing compensation packages.

195
00:10:29,190 --> 00:10:29,970
It's a real mess.

196
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:31,440
You're coming out loud.

197
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:32,270
How fast are we losing money?

198
00:10:34,550 --> 00:10:35,270
How can I put this?

199
00:10:35,890 --> 00:10:40,590
Let's just say that at this pace, we will be filing for Chapter 11 in less than three months.

200
00:10:40,790 --> 00:10:40,970
What?

201
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:43,610
Jeez, how could this have happened?

202
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:45,670
So what's the bad news?

203
00:10:46,090 --> 00:10:47,510
Oh, that's the really bad news.

204
00:10:47,970 --> 00:10:51,790
Our suppliers suffered QC problems and well, half of our production is faulty.

205
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:57,950
We're going to have to recall all items sold in the last quarter and the worst part, we're going to have to shoulder this cost.

206
00:10:58,590 --> 00:10:59,250
Are you joking?

207
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:01,610
Get the supplier on the line now.

208
00:11:02,110 --> 00:11:03,690
They have to assume the cost of this mess.

209
00:11:04,050 --> 00:11:04,990
We tried that, sir.

210
00:11:05,510 --> 00:11:08,690
The factory has gone under and the owner apparently has fled the country.

211
00:11:09,230 --> 00:11:09,450
We're doomed.

212
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:11,540
There's some really good news though.

213
00:11:12,040 --> 00:11:13,040
Really?

214
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:14,040
What?

215
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:15,040
I got offered a new job.

216
00:11:20,460 --> 00:11:21,080
All right, great.

217
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:31,880
So this topic of filing for Chapter 11 is actually very recent, very common now because recently one of the biggest companies filed for Chapter 11 in the US.

218
00:11:31,960 --> 00:11:32,560
Yeah, that's right.

219
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:39,240
But I often get confused by the phrase to file for Chapter 11 versus to file for Chapter 13.

220
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:45,840
All right, so in the United States, the bankruptcy law has different chapters or different sections, right?

221
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:57,360
And Chapter 11 is basically for companies that run out of money, that go bankrupt, so they have to declare themselves in bankruptcy and so they file for Chapter 11.

222
00:11:57,360 --> 00:11:59,080
But what about Chapter 13?

223
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:01,800
Chapter 13 is for people.

224
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:09,160
So people who have large debts, maybe with the car company or the telephone company and they can't pay, they file for Chapter 13.

225
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:12,120
They say, hey, I don't have money, I can't pay.

226
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:15,160
So I guess here it's kind of a metaphor again.

227
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:20,410
This phrase to file for Chapter 11 or Chapter 13 meaning bankruptcy.

228
00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:25,760
And people will say personally that they're filing for Chapter 11.

229
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:26,760
Exactly.

230
00:12:26,760 --> 00:12:33,070
Right, so this is an interesting situation because if you file for bankruptcy in some countries, you can go to jail.

231
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:36,560
I read recently about this happening in Dubai actually.

232
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:36,770
Exactly.

233
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:39,280
So there is prison for debts.

234
00:12:39,290 --> 00:12:41,690
In the US, you don't go to prison for debts.

235
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:45,640
But what about in your country's listeners?

236
00:12:45,640 --> 00:12:47,440
What happens when you go into debt in your country?

237
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:49,470
Or what happens when a company goes into bankruptcy?

238
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:51,640
Let us know.

239
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:52,640
Come to our website, EnglishPod.com.

240
00:12:53,290 --> 00:12:54,310
Leave your questions and comments.

241
00:12:54,680 --> 00:12:57,090
And of course, Erica and I are always there to help you out.

242
00:12:57,640 --> 00:13:00,090
Thanks for downloading you guys and until next time, goodbye.

243
00:13:00,160 --> 00:13:01,160
Bye.

244
00:13:01,160 --> 00:13:09,812
Bye-bye.
